---

## Overview

This hands-on lab introduces the fundamental concepts and commands of **Git version control**. It covers Git configuration, editor setup, repository initialization, file tracking, committing changes, and synchronization with a remote repository.

---

## Objectives

By completing this hands-on lab, the following objectives are achieved:

|  #  | Objective                                                   |
| :-: | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
|  01 | Verify the Git installation                                 |
|  02 | Configure Git username and email                            |
|  03 | Configure Notepad++ as the default Git editor               |
|  04 | Create and initialize a local Git repository                |
|  05 | Create and track files using Git                            |
|  06 | Stage and commit changes                                    |
|  07 | Connect the local repository to a remote repository         |
|  08 | Push and pull changes between local and remote repositories |

---

## Prerequisites

| Requirement             | Description                                |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| **Git**                 | Git must be installed on the local machine |
| **Git Bash**            | Required to execute Git commands           |
| **Notepad++**           | Used as the default Git text editor        |
| **GitHub / GitLab**     | Required for creating a remote repository  |
| **Internet Connection** | Required for remote repository operations  |

---

## 1. Git Configuration

### 1.1 Verify Git Installation

Open **Git Bash** and execute:

```bash
git --version
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
git version 2.xx.x.windows.x
```

</details>

> **Note:** If a valid Git version is displayed, Git has been installed successfully and is accessible from the command line.

---

### 1.2 Configure Git Username

Configure the username that will be associated with Git commits:

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
```

**Example:**

```bash
git config --global user.name "Rahul Sharma"
```

---

### 1.3 Configure Git Email Address

Configure the email address associated with the GitHub/GitLab account:

```bash
git config --global user.email "your_email@example.com"
```

**Example:**

```bash
git config --global user.email "rahul@gmail.com"
```

> **Tip:** Using the same email address as the GitHub account allows commits to be correctly associated with the GitHub profile.

---

### 1.4 Verify Git Configuration

Execute:

```bash
git config --global --list
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
user.name=Rahul Sharma
user.email=rahul@gmail.com
```

</details>

---

## 2. Configure Notepad++ as Git Editor

### 2.1 Verify Notepad++ Installation

Execute:

```bash
notepad++
```

If Notepad++ opens successfully, it is accessible from the system PATH.

If the command is not recognized, the default installation directory can be added to the Windows Environment Variables:

```text
C:\Program Files\Notepad++
```

---

### 2.2 Set Notepad++ as Default Git Editor

Execute:

```bash
git config --global core.editor "'C:/Program Files/Notepad++/notepad++.exe' -multiInst -nosession"
```

---

### 2.3 Verify the Editor Configuration

Execute:

```bash
git config --global core.editor
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
'C:/Program Files/Notepad++/notepad++.exe' -multiInst -nosession
```

</details>

> **Result:** Notepad++ is now configured as the default editor for Git operations.

---

## 3. Create a Local Git Repository

### 3.1 Create the Project Directory

Create a new directory named `GitDemo`:

```bash
mkdir GitDemo
```

Navigate into the directory:

```bash
cd GitDemo
```

---

### 3.2 Initialize the Repository

Execute:

```bash
git init
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
Initialized empty Git repository in .../GitDemo/.git/
```

</details>

The `GitDemo` directory is now initialized as a local Git repository.

---

## 4. Create and Track a File

### 4.1 Create `welcome.txt`

Create a new text file:

```bash
echo "Welcome to Git Hands-on Lab" > welcome.txt
```

The file contains:

```text
Welcome to Git Hands-on Lab
```

---

### 4.2 Check Repository Status

Execute:

```bash
git status
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
Untracked files:
  welcome.txt
```

</details>

The `welcome.txt` file is currently **untracked**, which means Git has detected the file but is not yet tracking its changes.

---

## 5. Stage and Commit the File

### 5.1 Add File to the Staging Area

Execute:

```bash
git add welcome.txt
```

Verify the status:

```bash
git status
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
Changes to be committed:
  new file: welcome.txt
```

</details>

---

### 5.2 Commit the Changes

Commit the staged file:

```bash
git commit -m "Add welcome.txt"
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
create mode 100644 welcome.txt
```

</details>

---

### 5.3 Verify Commit History

Execute:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

Example output:

```text
a1b2c3d Add welcome.txt
```

> The first value is the shortened **commit hash**, followed by the commit message.

---

## 6. Create a Remote Repository

Create a new repository named **GitDemo** on GitHub or GitLab.

For a clean initial push, create the remote repository without initializing it with:

* README
* `.gitignore`
* License

After creating the repository, copy its HTTPS URL.

**Example:**

```text
https://github.com/yourusername/GitDemo.git
```

---

## 7. Connect Local and Remote Repositories

### 7.1 Add the Remote Repository

Execute:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/GitDemo.git
```

Replace `yourusername` with the actual GitHub username.

---

### 7.2 Verify the Remote Repository

Execute:

```bash
git remote -v
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
origin  https://github.com/yourusername/GitDemo.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/yourusername/GitDemo.git (push)
```

</details>

The local repository is now connected to the remote repository using the remote name `origin`.

---

## 8. Configure the Main Branch

Rename the current branch to `main`:

```bash
git branch -M main
```

Verify:

```bash
git branch
```

**Expected Output:**

```text
* main
```

The `*` symbol represents the currently active branch.

---

## 9. Push Changes to the Remote Repository

Execute:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
branch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.
```

</details>

The local commits are now available in the remote GitHub/GitLab repository.

> **Note:** The `-u` option establishes an upstream tracking relationship. After the initial push, subsequent changes can normally be pushed using only `git push`.

---

## 10. Pull Changes from the Remote Repository

Execute:

```bash
git pull origin main
```

If the local repository already contains the latest changes:

```text
Already up to date.
```

The `git pull` command retrieves the latest changes from the remote repository and integrates them into the current local branch.

---

## 11. Final Verification

Check the repository status:

```bash
git status
```

<details>
<summary><b>View Expected Output</b></summary>

<br>

```text
On branch main
Your branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.

nothing to commit, working tree clean
```

</details>

Also open the **GitDemo** repository on GitHub/GitLab and verify that:

| Verification                                   | Expected Status |
| ---------------------------------------------- | :-------------: |
| Repository is accessible                       |        ✓        |
| `welcome.txt` is available                     |        ✓        |
| Commit is visible                              |        ✓        |
| `main` branch is available                     |        ✓        |
| Local and remote repositories are synchronized |        ✓        |

---

## Git Commands Reference

| Command                           | Description                                        |
| --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `git --version`                   | Displays the installed Git version                 |
| `git config --global user.name`   | Configures the Git username                        |
| `git config --global user.email`  | Configures the Git email address                   |
| `git config --global core.editor` | Configures the default Git editor                  |
| `git config --global --list`      | Displays the global Git configuration              |
| `git init`                        | Initializes a new Git repository                   |
| `git status`                      | Displays the current repository status             |
| `git add`                         | Adds changes to the staging area                   |
| `git commit`                      | Records staged changes in the repository           |
| `git log --oneline`               | Displays the commit history in compact format      |
| `git remote add origin`           | Connects a local repository to a remote repository |
| `git remote -v`                   | Displays configured remote repositories            |
| `git branch -M main`              | Renames the current branch to `main`               |
| `git push`                        | Uploads local commits to the remote repository     |
| `git pull`                        | Retrieves and integrates remote changes            |

---

## Result

The Git environment was successfully configured and the complete basic Git workflow was performed.

The following tasks were completed successfully:

* Git installation was verified.
* Git username and email were configured.
* Notepad++ was configured as the default Git editor.
* The `GitDemo` local repository was created and initialized.
* `welcome.txt` was created and added to source control.
* Changes were staged and committed successfully.
* The local repository was connected to a remote repository.
* The `main` branch was pushed to the remote repository.
* Pull operations were performed successfully.
* The final repository status was verified.

---

## Conclusion

This hands-on lab demonstrated the fundamental workflow of **Git version control**, including configuration, repository initialization, file tracking, staging, committing, and remote repository synchronization.

The exercise established a practical understanding of essential Git commands and provides the foundation required for subsequent concepts such as **`.gitignore`, branching, merging, merge conflict resolution, and collaborative version control workflows**.

